{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuUMXqAuXrgC2uoNQaW4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both now exist.\n\n**Data.** The Kibana Sample Data Logs view has these fields:\n- `timestamp`, the time field\n- `response`, a string, so I used `response.keyword`\n- `bytes`\n- `extension`\n- `clientip`\n- `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n- `machine.os`\n- `url` and `request`\n- `host`\n- `agent`\n\n**Dashboard.** \"EFF-d2eea73e Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by extension\n\nIt's at `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-d2eea73e-web-logs-overview-1c8d0c6184dc`. I haven't opened it to check how the panels render.\n\n**Alert rule.** \"EFF-d2eea73e 5xx spike\" (id `13a84179-249d-48c7-a9b0-1b962b1f987f`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every minute and counts documents where `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]`.\n- It fires when that count is above 10 within a 5-minute window.\n- I created it enabled, and its status was still \"pending\" when I created it. It has no action attached, so it will only show up as an alert in Kibana and won't notify anyone.\n- The sample data's timestamps may not be recent, so the rule may not fire until fresh data arrives."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":681,"cache_read_input_tokens":20354,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":681},"output_tokens":490,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0536cbf5-c97a-5467-80bd-e50c5d30a2ce","uuid":"3eef6f4a-681f-4589-b3db-57f6597d74a5","timestamp":"2026-10-11T00:05:05.341Z","request_id":"req_011CfuUMXdmT3pEuxeBzg8s2"}
